ally exposing port 8080 and enabling SSH, both required by the task.","evidence_ids":["cmd-752d00b310594e0b","cmd-e84c7b8b1b85451a","cmd-f70e48e62986417a","cmd-4d9f431431d44d4b","cmd-560034aa95124eec","cmd-a583cc9f41c54e5a","cmd-0618405474c54717","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: a rootless nginx container runs as unprivileged user appsvc on port 8080, is deployed and managed entirely via an Ansible playbook run from the correct control node (node2), and — critically — a real reboot of node1 was performed and the service is shown to have auto-started post-reboot with zero active login sessions for appsvc, satisfying the boot-persistence-without-login requirement with a genuine before/disruption/after transition rather than configuration alone. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed for both node1 and node2 with matching boot-id transitions corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good: the SSH/host-key fixes and initial playbook failure were legitimate prerequisites/corrections, fully cleaned up, with no residue in the final snapshots.","limitations":["The post-reboot recovery check for node1 (cmd-432306083f484b60) was captured only ~8 seconds after the boot_id change was confirmed, and the container had been up only ~5 seconds at that point — a longer settling window was not observed, though the evidence shown is sufficient to demonstrate the boot-time auto-start behavior.","The executor's ad hoc troubleshooting command outputs (e.g., journalctl during the ssh debugging) were truncated in places; only the final resolution state was directly verified.","No evidence was captured of the container correctly serving nginx content (only HTTP status code, not body), though this is a minor gap relative to the stated requirements."]},"ttft_ms":27967,"type":"